Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: A Betamax Swallows the Fairgrounds
- Story & Context: When the midday sun over the dirt track in Alliance, Nebraska shrank to a sliver of silver bone, Noah Vane jammed an old magnetic tape into a modified video deck wired directly into the Hammond B3’s preamp. As the sky plummeted into sudden twilight, thirty rusty carousel horses cast long purple penumbras across the dust while an arc of high-voltage ozone crackled from the Leslie horn, permanently welding the tonewheel gears to the audio head. In the pandemonium of screeching crows and frantic carnival barkers, Noah leaned into the drawbars and cried "the black coin takes the wheel" before the entire fairground evaporated into ten seconds of roaring, vacuum-packed feedback.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An abandoned, motionless carnival midway frozen under the surreal twilight of an eclipse, where a massive wooden Hammond organ sits in the center of the midway spewing endless reels of unspooled magnetic tape across the dry dirt, illuminated by a pale solar corona.

Track 2: Did You Tape Over the Corona?
- Story & Context: Crouched beneath the striped canvas awning of the funhouse as the temperature plummeted twenty degrees in four minutes, organist Lyra Haze wiped freezing condensation from the yellowed ivory keys with the torn silk of her ringmaster sleeve. Her confession hissed into a handheld Dictaphone while the Leslie cabinet wheezed like a dying steam engine, capturing her frantic drawbar adjustments as she realized she had accidentally recorded over the only master tape of the 1999 Munich totality. With cold tears stinging her cheeks and the wind smelling of spun sugar and burnt wire, she sang through the doppler horn, "tell the midway I forgot how daylight tastes," refusing to let go of the low pedal tone until the sun ruptured the sky.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro shot of frost and condensation coating wooden drawbars and vintage ivory organ keys, surrounded by tangled spools of cassette ribbon and glowing vacuum tubes, backlit by the blinding diamond-ring burst of an emerging sun.

Track 3: Silas Thorne at the Apogee Ferris
- Story & Context: Years after the great shadow swept east over the Atlantic, pilgrims still wander into the overgrown soybean fields outside Hopkinsville where Silas Thorne allegedly rode the highest gondola of an unpowered Ferris wheel straight into low-Earth orbit while holding down a full-drawbar cluster. The locals swear the rusted iron gears still turn on their own whenever an eclipse crosses the southern hemisphere, emitting a shuddering forty-hertz drone that tastes like copper pennies on the back of the tongue. Deep inside the bird-nested wooden ticket booth, an abandoned eight-track cartridge still loops the dying hum of the Hammond's vacuum tubes, chanting "we left the ticket booth among the stars" to the vacant wind.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A colossal, rusting Ferris wheel floating silently in the blackness of deep planetary space, its passenger cages glowing with dim fairy lights and trailing thick electrical umbilicals down to an antique wooden organ console drifting among stardust.

